{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0168q2uYBad2eyBwKbgSiMdo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2024/06/06/10-years-of-kubernetes/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project blog\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\", \"D1 Core Concepts\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"kubernetes-history\", \"borg\", \"first-commit-2014\", \"docker-2013\", \"go\", \"cncf-founding\", \"helmsman\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# 10 Years of Kubernetes (kubernetes.io/blog/2024/06/06/10-years-of-kubernetes/)\n9\t\n10\tTen (10) years ago, on June 6th, 2014, the first commit of Kubernetes was pushed to GitHub. That first commit with 250 files and 47,501 lines of go, bash and markdown kicked off the project we have today. Who could have predicted that 10 years later, Kubernetes would grow to become one of the largest Open Source projects to date with over 88,000 contributors from more than 8,000 companies, across 44 countries.\n11\t\n12\tKubernetes drew on Google's internal container-orchestration experience — the Borg system (and its research successor Omega) — and was written in Go. In March of 2013, a 5-minute lightning talk called \"The future of Linux Containers,\" presented by Solomon Hykes at PyCon, introduced an upcoming open source tool called \"Docker\" for creating and using Linux Containers; Docker's popularity set the stage for a system to orchestrate containers at scale. Kubernetes was announced publicly at DockerCon in June 2014, reached v1.0 in July 2015, and was donated by Google to the newly formed Cloud Native Computing Foundation (founded 2015 under the Linux Foundation) as its first project. The name comes from the Greek word for helmsman or pilot; \"K8s\" is the numeronym (eight letters between the K and the s). The ecosystem now spans close to 200 CNCF projects with 240,000+ individual contributors.\n13\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"7444727f-c812-4a7d-a7ce-0a0f442f066f","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:34:17.998Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-history-ten-years-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2024/06/06/10-years-of-kubernetes/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:40:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project blog\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles\", \"D1 Core Concepts\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"kubernetes-history\", \"borg\", \"first-commit-2014\", \"docker-2013\", \"go\", \"cncf-founding\", \"helmsman\"]\n---\n# 10 Years of Kubernetes (kubernetes.io/blog/2024/06/06/10-years-of-kubernetes/)\n\nTen (10) years ago, on June 6th, 2014, the first commit of Kubernetes was pushed to GitHub. That first commit with 250 files and 47,501 lines of go, bash and markdown kicked off the project we have today. Who could have predicted that 10 years later, Kubernetes would grow to become one of the largest Open Source projects to date with over 88,000 contributors from more than 8,000 companies, across 44 countries.\n\nKubernetes drew on Google's internal container-orchestration experience — the Borg system (and its research successor Omega) — and was written in Go. In March of 2013, a 5-minute lightning talk called \"The future of Linux Containers,\" presented by Solomon Hykes at PyCon, introduced an upcoming open source tool called \"Docker\" for creating and using Linux Containers; Docker's popularity set the stage for a system to orchestrate containers at scale. Kubernetes was announced publicly at DockerCon in June 2014, reached v1.0 in July 2015, and was donated by Google to the newly formed Cloud Native Computing Foundation (founded 2015 under the Linux Foundation) as its first project. The name comes from the Greek word for helmsman or pilot; \"K8s\" is the numeronym (eight letters between the K and the s). The ecosystem now spans close to 200 CNCF projects with 240,000+ individual contributors.\n","numLines":13,"startLine":1,"totalLines":13}}}
