{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XRLpAyVRFcHPqAnZaZ13tT","type":"tool_result","content":"# Immutable Infrastructure\n\n**Tags:** Infrastructure, Property\n\n\"Immutable Infrastructure refers to computer infrastructure (virtual machines, containers, network appliances) that cannot be changed once deployed.\" This enforcement occurs either through automated processes that overwrite unauthorized modifications or systems that prevent changes altogether.\n\nContainers exemplify this concept—persistent alterations require creating new container versions or recreating from images.\n\n**Security and Operational Benefits**\n\nBy blocking or detecting unauthorized modifications, immutable systems help organizations identify and address security vulnerabilities more effectively. \"Operating such a system becomes a lot more straightforward because administrators can make assumptions about it.\"\n\n**Integration with Infrastructure as Code**\n\nThis approach complements infrastructure as code practices, where automation scripts reside in version control systems like Git. \"This combination of immutability and version control means that there is a durable audit log of every authorized change to a system.\"\n\nThe pairing creates comprehensive documentation of all sanctioned infrastructure modifications, enabling better governance and traceability."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"162d52e2-30f0-4d34-aba5-f4cde678ae28","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:35:31.139Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":47460,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Immutable Infrastructure\n\n**Tags:** Infrastructure, Property\n\n\"Immutable Infrastructure refers to computer infrastructure (virtual machines, containers, network appliances) that cannot be changed once deployed.\" This enforcement occurs either through automated processes that overwrite unauthorized modifications or systems that prevent changes altogether.\n\nContainers exemplify this concept—persistent alterations require creating new container versions or recreating from images.\n\n**Security and Operational Benefits**\n\nBy blocking or detecting unauthorized modifications, immutable systems help organizations identify and address security vulnerabilities more effectively. \"Operating such a system becomes a lot more straightforward because administrators can make assumptions about it.\"\n\n**Integration with Infrastructure as Code**\n\nThis approach complements infrastructure as code practices, where automation scripts reside in version control systems like Git. \"This combination of immutability and version control means that there is a durable audit log of every authorized change to a system.\"\n\nThe pairing creates comprehensive documentation of all sanctioned infrastructure modifications, enabling better governance and traceability.","durationMs":3268,"url":"https://glossary.cncf.io/immutable-infrastructure/"}}
