{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VKV8ZQJj33zEtVmrte9oo4","type":"tool_result","content":"# Node Autoscaling Summary\n\nBased on the Kubernetes documentation page, here's what you need to know about node autoscaling:\n\n## Core Concept\n\nNode autoscaling automatically provisions and consolidates nodes in your cluster to adapt to demand and optimize cost. It operates through two main mechanisms: **provisioning** (scale-up) and **consolidation** (scale-down).\n\n## Node Provisioning\n\n**Provisioning** adds new nodes when pods cannot be scheduled on existing nodes. This is triggered by:\n\n- **Pod scheduling constraints**: Resource requests, node affinity, inter-pod affinity, and storage requirements\n- **Autoscaler configuration constraints**: Pre-defined node configurations or auto-provisioning rules\n- **Configured triggers**: Minimum node limits\n\nThe autoscaler considers pod requirements and ensures provisioned nodes have sufficient resources to accommodate pending pods.\n\n## Node Consolidation\n\n**Consolidation** removes underutilized nodes to improve cost-effectiveness by:\n\n- Removing empty nodes (those with only DaemonSet and static pods)\n- Consolidating non-empty nodes by evicting pods that can reschedule elsewhere\n- Optionally provisioning replacement nodes\n\nImportant: Consolidation should not leave pods in a pending state—all evicted pods must be able to reschedule on remaining or newly provisioned nodes.\n\n## How Autoscalers Work\n\nNode autoscalers interact with both Kubernetes APIs and cloud provider APIs to:\n\n1. Read pod and node status from Kubernetes\n2. Drain nodes (for consolidation)\n3. Create/remove cloud provider resources (typically virtual machines)\n\n## Named Implementations\n\nTwo main SIG Autoscaling-sponsored implementations exist:\n\n1. **Cluster Autoscaler**: Manages pre-configured node groups, provisioning to the best-fitting group and selecting specific nodes for removal\n2. **Karpenter**: Provides dynamic node configuration and additional features\n\nBoth provide similar core functionality with different architectural approaches and feature sets."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"1a6e2006-e6ae-4058-935e-3f9a806195a5","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:36:15.804Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":511149,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Node Autoscaling Summary\n\nBased on the Kubernetes documentation page, here's what you need to know about node autoscaling:\n\n## Core Concept\n\nNode autoscaling automatically provisions and consolidates nodes in your cluster to adapt to demand and optimize cost. It operates through two main mechanisms: **provisioning** (scale-up) and **consolidation** (scale-down).\n\n## Node Provisioning\n\n**Provisioning** adds new nodes when pods cannot be scheduled on existing nodes. This is triggered by:\n\n- **Pod scheduling constraints**: Resource requests, node affinity, inter-pod affinity, and storage requirements\n- **Autoscaler configuration constraints**: Pre-defined node configurations or auto-provisioning rules\n- **Configured triggers**: Minimum node limits\n\nThe autoscaler considers pod requirements and ensures provisioned nodes have sufficient resources to accommodate pending pods.\n\n## Node Consolidation\n\n**Consolidation** removes underutilized nodes to improve cost-effectiveness by:\n\n- Removing empty nodes (those with only DaemonSet and static pods)\n- Consolidating non-empty nodes by evicting pods that can reschedule elsewhere\n- Optionally provisioning replacement nodes\n\nImportant: Consolidation should not leave pods in a pending state—all evicted pods must be able to reschedule on remaining or newly provisioned nodes.\n\n## How Autoscalers Work\n\nNode autoscalers interact with both Kubernetes APIs and cloud provider APIs to:\n\n1. Read pod and node status from Kubernetes\n2. Drain nodes (for consolidation)\n3. Create/remove cloud provider resources (typically virtual machines)\n\n## Named Implementations\n\nTwo main SIG Autoscaling-sponsored implementations exist:\n\n1. **Cluster Autoscaler**: Manages pre-configured node groups, provisioning to the best-fitting group and selecting specific nodes for removal\n2. **Karpenter**: Provides dynamic node configuration and additional features\n\nBoth provide similar core functionality with different architectural approaches and feature sets.","durationMs":6983,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/node-autoscaling/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0197VayFCXUhK1dRehwhF2BZ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Karpenter documentation node autoscaler what is Karpenter\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Karpenter\",\"url\":\"https://karpenter.sh/docs/concepts/\"},{\"title\":\"Migrating from Cluster Autoscaler\",\"url\":\"https://karpenter.sh/docs/getting-started/migrating-from-cas/\"},{\"title\":\"Node Autoscaling | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/node-autoscaling/\"},{\"title\":\"Introducing Headlamp Plugin for Karpenter - Scaling and Visibility | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/10/06/introducing-headlamp-plugin-for-karpenter/\"},{\"title\":\"Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://karpenter.sh/docs/\"},{\"title\":\"Getting Started with Karpenter\",\"url\":\"https://karpenter.sh/docs/getting-started/getting-started-with-karpenter/\"},{\"title\":\"Running Karpenter and AKS Cluster Autoscaler Together for Optimized Node Scaling in AKS - General Discussions - Discuss Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://discuss.kubernetes.io/t/running-karpenter-and-aks-cluster-autoscaler-together-for-optimized-node-scaling-in-aks/31881\"},{\"title\":\"Kubernetes v1.37: Garhwal | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2026/08/26/kubernetes-v1-37-release/\"},{\"title\":\"Overprovision Node Capacity For A Cluster | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/node-overprovisioning/\"},{\"title\":\"Kubernetes 1.35: In-Place Pod Resize Graduates to Stable | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/12/19/kubernetes-v1-35-in-place-pod-resize-ga/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what Karpenter is:\n\n## What is Karpenter?\n\nKarpenter is an open-source node lifecycle management project built for Kubernetes. It is a Kubernetes Autoscaling SIG node provisioning project that helps clusters scale quickly and efficiently.\n\n## How Karpenter Works\n\nKarpenter works by: Watching for pods that the Kubernetes scheduler has marked as unschedulable, Evaluating scheduling constraints (resource requests, nodeselectors, affinities, tolerations, and topology spread constraints) requested by the pods, Provisioning nodes that meet the requirements of the pods, and Disrupting the nodes when the nodes are no longer needed.\n\n## Key Features\n\n**Quick Node Provisioning:** Karpenter manages each instance directly, without use of additional orchestration mechanisms like node groups. This enables it to retry in milliseconds instead of minutes when capacity is unavailable.\n\n**Full Cloud Flexibility:** Karpenter has the ability to efficiently address the full range of instance types available through AWS.\n\n**Full Lifecycle Management:** Karpenter handles all aspects of node lifecycle, not just autoscaling. This includes automatically refreshing Nodes once they reach a certain lifetime, and auto-upgrading Nodes when new worker Node images are released.\n\n## NodePools\n\nA NodePool defines constraints that determine which nodes can be provisioned and specifies the attributes these nodes will have. A single Karpenter NodePool is capable of handling many different pod shapes. Karpenter makes scheduling and provisioning decisions based on pod attributes such as labels and affinity. In other words, Karpenter eliminates the need to manage many different node groups.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"fdc27fd6-dd8b-4a36-9dc8-a9fe09e8b9bf","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:36:17.642Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Karpenter documentation node autoscaler what is Karpenter","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01R6rtNHzrQjkqgezCJ3wWDH","content":[{"title":"Karpenter","url":"https://karpenter.sh/docs/concepts/"},{"title":"Migrating from Cluster Autoscaler","url":"https://karpenter.sh/docs/getting-started/migrating-from-cas/"},{"title":"Node Autoscaling | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/node-autoscaling/"},{"title":"Introducing Headlamp Plugin for Karpenter - Scaling and Visibility | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/10/06/introducing-headlamp-plugin-for-karpenter/"},{"title":"Documentation","url":"https://karpenter.sh/docs/"},{"title":"Getting Started with Karpenter","url":"https://karpenter.sh/docs/getting-started/getting-started-with-karpenter/"},{"title":"Running Karpenter and AKS Cluster Autoscaler Together for Optimized Node Scaling in AKS - General Discussions - Discuss Kubernetes","url":"https://discuss.kubernetes.io/t/running-karpenter-and-aks-cluster-autoscaler-together-for-optimized-node-scaling-in-aks/31881"},{"title":"Kubernetes v1.37: Garhwal | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2026/08/26/kubernetes-v1-37-release/"},{"title":"Overprovision Node Capacity For A Cluster | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/node-overprovisioning/"},{"title":"Kubernetes 1.35: In-Place Pod Resize Graduates to Stable | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/12/19/kubernetes-v1-35-in-place-pod-resize-ga/"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what Karpenter is:\n\n## What is Karpenter?\n\nKarpenter is an open-source node lifecycle management project built for Kubernetes. It is a Kubernetes Autoscaling SIG node provisioning project that helps clusters scale quickly and efficiently.\n\n## How Karpenter Works\n\nKarpenter works by: Watching for pods that the Kubernetes scheduler has marked as unschedulable, Evaluating scheduling constraints (resource requests, nodeselectors, affinities, tolerations, and topology spread constraints) requested by the pods, Provisioning nodes that meet the requirements of the pods, and Disrupting the nodes when the nodes are no longer needed.\n\n## Key Features\n\n**Quick Node Provisioning:** Karpenter manages each instance directly, without use of additional orchestration mechanisms like node groups. This enables it to retry in milliseconds instead of minutes when capacity is unavailable.\n\n**Full Cloud Flexibility:** Karpenter has the ability to efficiently address the full range of instance types available through AWS.\n\n**Full Lifecycle Management:** Karpenter handles all aspects of node lifecycle, not just autoscaling. This includes automatically refreshing Nodes once they reach a certain lifetime, and auto-upgrading Nodes when new worker Node images are released.\n\n## NodePools\n\nA NodePool defines constraints that determine which nodes can be provisioned and specifies the attributes these nodes will have. A single Karpenter NodePool is capable of handling many different pod shapes. Karpenter makes scheduling and provisioning decisions based on pod attributes such as labels and affinity. In other words, Karpenter eliminates the need to manage many different node groups."],"durationSeconds":8.66424979999999,"searchCount":1}}
