{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NtjRDTeNLTA2i7QTYp7MRR","type":"tool_result","content":"# Node Autoscaling - Key Definitions\n\n## (1) Opening Definition of Node Autoscaling\n\n\"Automatically provision and consolidate the Nodes in your cluster to adapt to demand and optimize cost.\"\n\n## (2) Provisioning Definition and Trigger\n\n\"If there are Pods in a cluster that can't be scheduled on existing Nodes, new Nodes can be automatically added to the cluster—_provisioned_—to accommodate the Pods.\"\n\nThe exact sentence naming what triggers provisioning: \"If there are Pods in a cluster that can't be scheduled on existing Nodes, new Nodes can be automatically added to the cluster—_provisioned_—to accommodate the Pods.\"\n\n## (3) Consolidation Definition\n\n\"Nodes in your cluster can be automatically _consolidated_ in order to improve the overall Node utilization, and in turn the cost-effectiveness of the cluster. Consolidation happens through removing a set of underutilized Nodes from the cluster.\"\n\n## (4) Cluster Autoscaler and Karpenter Descriptions\n\n\"[Cluster Autoscaler](https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/tree/master/cluster-autoscaler) and [Karpenter](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/karpenter) are the two Node autoscalers currently sponsored by [SIG Autoscaling](https://github.com/kubernetes/community/tree/main/sig-autoscaling).\"\n\n**Cluster Autoscaler:** \"Cluster Autoscaler adds or removes Nodes to pre-configured _Node groups_.\"\n\n**Karpenter:** The content was truncated before the full Karpenter description could be captured in the provided excerpt."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"c53f8ded-1e2d-4ad9-9925-6ea2453c752a","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:36:29.013Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":511149,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Node Autoscaling - Key Definitions\n\n## (1) Opening Definition of Node Autoscaling\n\n\"Automatically provision and consolidate the Nodes in your cluster to adapt to demand and optimize cost.\"\n\n## (2) Provisioning Definition and Trigger\n\n\"If there are Pods in a cluster that can't be scheduled on existing Nodes, new Nodes can be automatically added to the cluster—_provisioned_—to accommodate the Pods.\"\n\nThe exact sentence naming what triggers provisioning: \"If there are Pods in a cluster that can't be scheduled on existing Nodes, new Nodes can be automatically added to the cluster—_provisioned_—to accommodate the Pods.\"\n\n## (3) Consolidation Definition\n\n\"Nodes in your cluster can be automatically _consolidated_ in order to improve the overall Node utilization, and in turn the cost-effectiveness of the cluster. Consolidation happens through removing a set of underutilized Nodes from the cluster.\"\n\n## (4) Cluster Autoscaler and Karpenter Descriptions\n\n\"[Cluster Autoscaler](https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/tree/master/cluster-autoscaler) and [Karpenter](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/karpenter) are the two Node autoscalers currently sponsored by [SIG Autoscaling](https://github.com/kubernetes/community/tree/main/sig-autoscaling).\"\n\n**Cluster Autoscaler:** \"Cluster Autoscaler adds or removes Nodes to pre-configured _Node groups_.\"\n\n**Karpenter:** The content was truncated before the full Karpenter description could be captured in the provided excerpt.","durationMs":4799,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/node-autoscaling/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019FBS2XNmzafv7VXAdh9oR1","type":"tool_result","content":"# Karpenter: Exact Quotes from Documentation\n\n**What Karpenter is:**\nThe page does not provide a single definitive sentence defining what Karpenter is as a whole.\n\n**What Karpenter does (bulleted list):**\n\"Karpenter's job is to add nodes to handle unschedulable pods, schedule pods on those nodes, and remove the nodes when they are not needed.\"\n\n**What a NodePool is:**\n\"Karpenter defines a Custom Resource called a NodePool to specify configuration.\"\n\n**SIG or organizational affiliation:**\nThe page indicates Karpenter is associated with Kubernetes through the GitHub repository reference \"kubernetes-sigs/karpenter\" but contains no explicit textual statement about SIG membership. The page notes it's \"built with ❤️ at AWS\" and is subject to \"© 2026 Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"dd78d113-af67-47f4-a8b4-6605752a2d0d","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:36:29.216Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":37141,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Karpenter: Exact Quotes from Documentation\n\n**What Karpenter is:**\nThe page does not provide a single definitive sentence defining what Karpenter is as a whole.\n\n**What Karpenter does (bulleted list):**\n\"Karpenter's job is to add nodes to handle unschedulable pods, schedule pods on those nodes, and remove the nodes when they are not needed.\"\n\n**What a NodePool is:**\n\"Karpenter defines a Custom Resource called a NodePool to specify configuration.\"\n\n**SIG or organizational affiliation:**\nThe page indicates Karpenter is associated with Kubernetes through the GitHub repository reference \"kubernetes-sigs/karpenter\" but contains no explicit textual statement about SIG membership. The page notes it's \"built with ❤️ at AWS\" and is subject to \"© 2026 Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates.\"","durationMs":3589,"url":"https://karpenter.sh/docs/concepts/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GnaPe6PwAHfpBV2WrsZxsE","type":"tool_result","content":"# Istio Security: Key Concepts\n\n**Security Overview Goals:**\n\"The goals of Istio security are: Security by default: no changes needed to application code and infrastructure; Defense in depth: integrate with existing security systems to provide multiple layers of defense; Zero-trust network: build security solutions on distrusted networks.\"\n\n**High-Level Architecture:**\n\"Security in Istio involves multiple components: A Certificate Authority (CA) for key and certificate management; The configuration API server distributes to the proxies: authentication policies, authorization policies, secure naming information; Sidecar and perimeter proxies work as Policy Enforcement Points (PEPs) to secure communication between clients and servers.\"\n\n**Istio Identity:**\n\"The Istio identity model uses the first-class `service identity` to determine the identity of a request's origin. This model allows for great flexibility and granularity for service identities to represent a human user, an individual workload, or a group of workloads.\"\n\n**Mutual TLS Authentication Process:**\n\"When a workload sends a request to another workload using mutual TLS authentication, the request is handled as follows: 1) Istio re-routes the outbound traffic from a client to the client's local sidecar Envoy; 2) The client side Envoy starts a mutual TLS handshake with the server side Envoy...3) The client side Envoy and the server side Envoy establish a mutual TLS connection; 4) The server side Envoy authorizes the request.\"\n\n**Permissive Mode:**\n\"With the permissive mode enabled, the server accepts both plaintext and mutual TLS traffic. The mode provides greater flexibility for the on-boarding process.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"6616e217-d28e-40a1-bb48-b835e4207cfb","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:36:33.721Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":157977,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Istio Security: Key Concepts\n\n**Security Overview Goals:**\n\"The goals of Istio security are: Security by default: no changes needed to application code and infrastructure; Defense in depth: integrate with existing security systems to provide multiple layers of defense; Zero-trust network: build security solutions on distrusted networks.\"\n\n**High-Level Architecture:**\n\"Security in Istio involves multiple components: A Certificate Authority (CA) for key and certificate management; The configuration API server distributes to the proxies: authentication policies, authorization policies, secure naming information; Sidecar and perimeter proxies work as Policy Enforcement Points (PEPs) to secure communication between clients and servers.\"\n\n**Istio Identity:**\n\"The Istio identity model uses the first-class `service identity` to determine the identity of a request's origin. This model allows for great flexibility and granularity for service identities to represent a human user, an individual workload, or a group of workloads.\"\n\n**Mutual TLS Authentication Process:**\n\"When a workload sends a request to another workload using mutual TLS authentication, the request is handled as follows: 1) Istio re-routes the outbound traffic from a client to the client's local sidecar Envoy; 2) The client side Envoy starts a mutual TLS handshake with the server side Envoy...3) The client side Envoy and the server side Envoy establish a mutual TLS connection; 4) The server side Envoy authorizes the request.\"\n\n**Permissive Mode:**\n\"With the permissive mode enabled, the server accepts both plaintext and mutual TLS traffic. The mode provides greater flexibility for the on-boarding process.\"","durationMs":6384,"url":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/"}}
