{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011L9a6MTN5cfuy9vrfijKAQ","type":"tool_result","content":"# Knative Serving Autoscaling Overview\n\n**What the autoscaler is:**\n\"Knative Serving provides automatic scaling, or _autoscaling_, for applications to match incoming demand.\"\n\n**Default autoscaler:**\n\"This is provided by default, by using the Knative Pod Autoscaler (KPA).\"\n\n**How scale-to-zero works:**\n\"If an application is receiving no traffic and scale to zero is enabled, Knative Serving scales the application down to zero replicas.\"\n\n**Alternative autoscaler option:**\nThe documentation mentions the possibility to \"Configure your Knative deployment to use the Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) instead of the default KPA.\"\n\n**Note:** The provided content does not include a description of a \"request-driven scaling model.\" The document focuses on demand-based scaling and scale-to-zero functionality but does not explicitly describe this specific terminology."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"e8676dd2-2786-4521-a408-85d0aeaf3539","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:37:15.233Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":170378,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Knative Serving Autoscaling Overview\n\n**What the autoscaler is:**\n\"Knative Serving provides automatic scaling, or _autoscaling_, for applications to match incoming demand.\"\n\n**Default autoscaler:**\n\"This is provided by default, by using the Knative Pod Autoscaler (KPA).\"\n\n**How scale-to-zero works:**\n\"If an application is receiving no traffic and scale to zero is enabled, Knative Serving scales the application down to zero replicas.\"\n\n**Alternative autoscaler option:**\nThe documentation mentions the possibility to \"Configure your Knative deployment to use the Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) instead of the default KPA.\"\n\n**Note:** The provided content does not include a description of a \"request-driven scaling model.\" The document focuses on demand-based scaling and scale-to-zero functionality but does not explicitly describe this specific terminology.","durationMs":3143,"url":"https://knative.dev/docs/serving/autoscaling/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vy4s5SBNunUFrHseSYAAG7","type":"tool_result","content":"# Vertical Pod Autoscaler Description\n\nBased on the README content, here are the exact quotes:\n\n**What VPA is and does:**\n\"Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) frees users from the necessity of setting up-to-date resource requests for the containers in their pods. When configured, it will set the requests automatically based on usage and thus allow proper scheduling onto nodes so that appropriate resource overhead is available for each pod. It will also maintain ratios between requests and limits that were specified in initial containers configuration.\"\n\n**Its capabilities:**\n\"It can both down-scale pods that are over-requesting resources, and also up-scale pods that are under-requesting resources based on their usage over time.\"\n\n**Components:**\n\"The Vertical Pod Autoscaler consists of three parts. The recommender, updater and admission-controller.\"\n\n**Known limitation regarding pod-level resources:**\nThe document notes VPA currently has incompatibility issues with pod-level resource definitions, but contains no explicit statement about separate installation requirements or in-place resize support."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"a21a3d57-c3ba-4bb8-8b46-50d6bfabf614","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:37:17.788Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":263468,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Vertical Pod Autoscaler Description\n\nBased on the README content, here are the exact quotes:\n\n**What VPA is and does:**\n\"Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) frees users from the necessity of setting up-to-date resource requests for the containers in their pods. When configured, it will set the requests automatically based on usage and thus allow proper scheduling onto nodes so that appropriate resource overhead is available for each pod. It will also maintain ratios between requests and limits that were specified in initial containers configuration.\"\n\n**Its capabilities:**\n\"It can both down-scale pods that are over-requesting resources, and also up-scale pods that are under-requesting resources based on their usage over time.\"\n\n**Components:**\n\"The Vertical Pod Autoscaler consists of three parts. The recommender, updater and admission-controller.\"\n\n**Known limitation regarding pod-level resources:**\nThe document notes VPA currently has incompatibility issues with pod-level resource definitions, but contains no explicit statement about separate installation requirements or in-place resize support.","durationMs":3946,"url":"https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler/blob/master/vertical-pod-autoscaler/README.md"}}
