{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016PNUek5eQ3QH84gZopv2wH","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CNCF Technical Advisory Groups TAG list 2026 restructured TAG Workloads Foundation TAG Operational Resilience\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"TAG Operational Resilience | CNCF Contributors\",\"url\":\"https://contribute.cncf.io/community/tags/operational-resilience/\"},{\"title\":\"cncf-tag-operational-resilience@lists.cncf.io | Home\",\"url\":\"https://lists.cncf.io/g/cncf-tag-operational-resilience\"},{\"title\":\"Technical Advisory Groups (TAGs) | CNCF Contributors\",\"url\":\"https://contribute.cncf.io/community/tags/\"},{\"title\":\"10 Years in Cloud Native: TOC Restructures Technical Groups | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2025/05/07/10-years-in-cloud-native-toc-restructures-technical-groups/\"},{\"title\":\"TAG Operational Resilience Charter - CNCF Contributors - Cloud ...\",\"url\":\"https://contribute.cncf.io/community/tags/operational-resilience/charter/\"},{\"title\":\"Three TAG leads walk into the TOC | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/05/26/three-tag-leads-walk-into-the-toc/\"},{\"title\":\"# TAG Operational Resilience Tech Lead Nomination · Issue #2178 · cncf/toc\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cncf/toc/issues/2178\"},{\"title\":\"TAG Workloads Foundation Tech Lead Nominations 2026 · Issue #2176 · cncf/toc\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/cncf/toc/issues/2176\"},{\"title\":\"cncf-tag-operational-resilience@lists.cncf.io | Subgroups\",\"url\":\"https://lists.cncf.io/g/cncf-tag-operational-resilience/subgroups\"},{\"title\":\"CNCF TAG Runtime\",\"url\":\"https://tag-runtime.cncf.io/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I can provide you with information about the CNCF Technical Advisory Groups (TAGs) restructuring and the specific TAGs you asked about:\n\n## CNCF TAG Restructuring in 2026\n\nThe TOC approved a restructuring of the Technical Advisory Groups, and other technical groups directly under the TOC structure. This restructuring is currently underway, and the March 18, 2025 TOC Update provides a mapping of previous TAGs and Working Groups to current TAGs, Subprojects and Initiatives.\n\n## TAG Workloads Foundation\n\nTAG Workloads Foundation is focused on defining practices and standards for cloud native workload execution within systems, applications, and architectures, with 3 Chairs and 5 Tech Leads and TOC Liaisons including Alex Chircop, Jeremy Rickard, Joseph Sandoval, and Chad Beaudin.\n\n## TAG Operational Resilience\n\nTAG Operational Resilience is dedicated to operational resilience and reliability practices in cloud native systems, covering observability, management, business continuity, resource optimization, cost efficiency, energy, performance, troubleshooting, reliability, and Day 2 operations. The TAG has 3 Chairs and 5 Tech Leads with TOC Liaisons including Chad Beaudin, Faseela K, Mauricio Salatino, Mario Fahlandt, and Jeremy Rickard.\n\nBoth of these TAGs are part of the 2026 CNCF restructuring and have active leadership elections and initiatives underway.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"3da3408d-ab32-460d-a11b-7d2183e724ae","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:37:31.425Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CNCF Technical Advisory Groups TAG list 2026 restructured TAG Workloads Foundation TAG Operational Resilience","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Kb28wen7G8p4BvphnQXqaZ","content":[{"title":"TAG Operational Resilience | CNCF Contributors","url":"https://contribute.cncf.io/community/tags/operational-resilience/"},{"title":"cncf-tag-operational-resilience@lists.cncf.io | Home","url":"https://lists.cncf.io/g/cncf-tag-operational-resilience"},{"title":"Technical Advisory Groups (TAGs) | CNCF Contributors","url":"https://contribute.cncf.io/community/tags/"},{"title":"10 Years in Cloud Native: TOC Restructures Technical Groups | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2025/05/07/10-years-in-cloud-native-toc-restructures-technical-groups/"},{"title":"TAG Operational Resilience Charter - CNCF Contributors - Cloud ...","url":"https://contribute.cncf.io/community/tags/operational-resilience/charter/"},{"title":"Three TAG leads walk into the TOC | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/05/26/three-tag-leads-walk-into-the-toc/"},{"title":"# TAG Operational Resilience Tech Lead Nomination · Issue #2178 · cncf/toc","url":"https://github.com/cncf/toc/issues/2178"},{"title":"TAG Workloads Foundation Tech Lead Nominations 2026 · Issue #2176 · cncf/toc","url":"https://github.com/cncf/toc/issues/2176"},{"title":"cncf-tag-operational-resilience@lists.cncf.io | Subgroups","url":"https://lists.cncf.io/g/cncf-tag-operational-resilience/subgroups"},{"title":"CNCF TAG Runtime","url":"https://tag-runtime.cncf.io/"}]},"Based on the search results, I can provide you with information about the CNCF Technical Advisory Groups (TAGs) restructuring and the specific TAGs you asked about:\n\n## CNCF TAG Restructuring in 2026\n\nThe TOC approved a restructuring of the Technical Advisory Groups, and other technical groups directly under the TOC structure. This restructuring is currently underway, and the March 18, 2025 TOC Update provides a mapping of previous TAGs and Working Groups to current TAGs, Subprojects and Initiatives.\n\n## TAG Workloads Foundation\n\nTAG Workloads Foundation is focused on defining practices and standards for cloud native workload execution within systems, applications, and architectures, with 3 Chairs and 5 Tech Leads and TOC Liaisons including Alex Chircop, Jeremy Rickard, Joseph Sandoval, and Chad Beaudin.\n\n## TAG Operational Resilience\n\nTAG Operational Resilience is dedicated to operational resilience and reliability practices in cloud native systems, covering observability, management, business continuity, resource optimization, cost efficiency, energy, performance, troubleshooting, reliability, and Day 2 operations. The TAG has 3 Chairs and 5 Tech Leads with TOC Liaisons including Chad Beaudin, Faseela K, Mauricio Salatino, Mario Fahlandt, and Jeremy Rickard.\n\nBoth of these TAGs are part of the 2026 CNCF restructuring and have active leadership elections and initiatives underway."],"durationSeconds":7.6105172999999775,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M1fVS7aAMhfzA6Snpfqjtu","type":"tool_result","content":"# CNCF End User Community and Technical Advisory Board\n\n**CNCF End User Community:**\n\"Where users of cloud native technology connect, collaborate, and lead.\"\n\n**End User Technical Advisory Board:**\n\"The End User TAB serves as the voice of the end users in CNCF community decisions\"\n\n**Relationship to TOC:**\nThe page does not explicitly describe the relationship between the End User TAB and the Technical Oversight Committee (TOC). It mentions that End User Contributors gain \"Eligibility to run for and serve on the End User Technical Advisory Board\" and separately lists the TOC as defining \"CNCF's technical vision,\" but does not detail how these two bodies interact or relate to each other."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"63afedbb-8cde-4dd2-b594-f441d7d1c661","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:37:32.249Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":181878,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CNCF End User Community and Technical Advisory Board\n\n**CNCF End User Community:**\n\"Where users of cloud native technology connect, collaborate, and lead.\"\n\n**End User Technical Advisory Board:**\n\"The End User TAB serves as the voice of the end users in CNCF community decisions\"\n\n**Relationship to TOC:**\nThe page does not explicitly describe the relationship between the End User TAB and the Technical Oversight Committee (TOC). It mentions that End User Contributors gain \"Eligibility to run for and serve on the End User Technical Advisory Board\" and separately lists the TOC as defining \"CNCF's technical vision,\" but does not detail how these two bodies interact or relate to each other.","durationMs":2655,"url":"https://www.cncf.io/enduser/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A19u2eAWmN2cdPseSCXwXQ","type":"tool_result","content":"# Kubernetes Release Cycle\n\n**Description of the release cycle:**\n\"The release process can be thought of as having three main phases:\n\n*   Enhancement Definition\n*   Implementation\n*   Stabilization\"\n\n**Release cadence:**\n\"Kubernetes releases currently happen approximately three times per year.\"\n\nThe release cycle is approximately 14+ weeks, with the main phases broken down as:\n- \"Normal Dev (Weeks 1-11)\"\n- \"Code Freeze (Weeks 12-14)\"\n- \"Post-Release (Weeks 14+)\"\n\n**Phases of the release cycle:**\n\n\"**[Enhancements Freeze](https://git.k8s.io/sig-release/releases/release_phases.md#enhancements-freeze)** starts ~4 weeks into release cycle.\"\n\n\"**[Code Freeze](https://git.k8s.io/sig-release/releases/release_phases.md#code-freeze)** starts in week ~12 and continues for ~2 weeks. Only critical bug fixes are accepted into the release codebase during this time.\"\n\n**SIG Release and Release Team statement:**\n\n\"The process for shepherding enhancements, issues, and pull requests into a Kubernetes release spans multiple stakeholders:\n\n*   the enhancement, issue, and pull request owner(s)\n*   SIG leadership\n*   the [Release Team](https://git.k8s.io/sig-release/release-team)\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"9d6288ea-4d35-48e5-8996-52ce68204fb8","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:37:32.779Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":65160,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kubernetes Release Cycle\n\n**Description of the release cycle:**\n\"The release process can be thought of as having three main phases:\n\n*   Enhancement Definition\n*   Implementation\n*   Stabilization\"\n\n**Release cadence:**\n\"Kubernetes releases currently happen approximately three times per year.\"\n\nThe release cycle is approximately 14+ weeks, with the main phases broken down as:\n- \"Normal Dev (Weeks 1-11)\"\n- \"Code Freeze (Weeks 12-14)\"\n- \"Post-Release (Weeks 14+)\"\n\n**Phases of the release cycle:**\n\n\"**[Enhancements Freeze](https://git.k8s.io/sig-release/releases/release_phases.md#enhancements-freeze)** starts ~4 weeks into release cycle.\"\n\n\"**[Code Freeze](https://git.k8s.io/sig-release/releases/release_phases.md#code-freeze)** starts in week ~12 and continues for ~2 weeks. Only critical bug fixes are accepted into the release codebase during this time.\"\n\n**SIG Release and Release Team statement:**\n\n\"The process for shepherding enhancements, issues, and pull requests into a Kubernetes release spans multiple stakeholders:\n\n*   the enhancement, issue, and pull request owner(s)\n*   SIG leadership\n*   the [Release Team](https://git.k8s.io/sig-release/release-team)\"","durationMs":5377,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/releases/release/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ExsE54t54xZJRNu55ycjTh","type":"tool_result","content":"# SIG Release Charter Overview\n\n**Scope Statement:**\n\"Production of Kubernetes releases on a reliable schedule\" and to \"Ensure there is a consistent group of community members in place to support the release process across time.\"\n\n**In-Scope Responsibilities:**\n\nRelease process management includes \"Defining and staffing release roles to manage the resolution of release blocking criteria\" and \"Defining and driving development processes (e.g. merge queues, cherrypicks) and release processes (e.g. burndown meetings, cutting pre-releases).\"\n\nRelease engineering encompasses \"Managing the creation of release specific artifacts, including: Code branches, Binary artifacts, Container Images, Release notes.\"\n\n**Subprojects:**\n\nThe document identifies one specific subproject: the \"Release Engineering subproject,\" which is \"dedicated to the technical aspects of Kubernetes releases, for example its tooling and source code ownership.\"\n\nThe charter does not provide a comprehensive list of other owned subprojects beyond this one explicitly named entity."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"0d00c943-2367-4940-9f3c-9e7f69397557","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:37:33.192Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":276276,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# SIG Release Charter Overview\n\n**Scope Statement:**\n\"Production of Kubernetes releases on a reliable schedule\" and to \"Ensure there is a consistent group of community members in place to support the release process across time.\"\n\n**In-Scope Responsibilities:**\n\nRelease process management includes \"Defining and staffing release roles to manage the resolution of release blocking criteria\" and \"Defining and driving development processes (e.g. merge queues, cherrypicks) and release processes (e.g. burndown meetings, cutting pre-releases).\"\n\nRelease engineering encompasses \"Managing the creation of release specific artifacts, including: Code branches, Binary artifacts, Container Images, Release notes.\"\n\n**Subprojects:**\n\nThe document identifies one specific subproject: the \"Release Engineering subproject,\" which is \"dedicated to the technical aspects of Kubernetes releases, for example its tooling and source code ownership.\"\n\nThe charter does not provide a comprehensive list of other owned subprojects beyond this one explicitly named entity.","durationMs":4376,"url":"https://github.com/kubernetes/community/blob/master/sig-release/charter.md"}}
