{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019fQ7ebT8YsPnNnXoDEgXVn","type":"tool_result","content":"# Kubernetes API Aggregation Layer - Exact Quotes\n\nBased on the documentation provided:\n\n**What the aggregation layer is:**\n\"The aggregation layer allows Kubernetes to be extended with additional APIs, beyond what is offered by the core Kubernetes APIs.\"\n\n**How it lets you extend Kubernetes:**\n\"The aggregation layer runs in-process with the kube-apiserver. Until an extension resource is registered, the aggregation layer will do nothing. To register an API, you add an _APIService_ object, which \"claims\" the URL path in the Kubernetes API. At that point, the aggregation layer will proxy anything sent to that API path (e.g. `/apis/myextension.mycompany.io/v1/…`) to the registered APIService.\"\n\n**What an APIService object registers:**\nAn APIService object \"claims\" the URL path in the Kubernetes API.\n\n**How it differs from CustomResourceDefinitions:**\n\"The aggregation layer is different from [Custom Resource Definitions](/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/api-extension/custom-resources/), which are a way to make the [kube-apiserver](/docs/concepts/architecture/#kube-apiserver \"Control plane component that serves the Kubernetes API.\") recognise new kinds of object.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"ad192cc4-dafe-4c41-bee6-52b8fc03b8be","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:38:15.484Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":496852,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kubernetes API Aggregation Layer - Exact Quotes\n\nBased on the documentation provided:\n\n**What the aggregation layer is:**\n\"The aggregation layer allows Kubernetes to be extended with additional APIs, beyond what is offered by the core Kubernetes APIs.\"\n\n**How it lets you extend Kubernetes:**\n\"The aggregation layer runs in-process with the kube-apiserver. Until an extension resource is registered, the aggregation layer will do nothing. To register an API, you add an _APIService_ object, which \"claims\" the URL path in the Kubernetes API. At that point, the aggregation layer will proxy anything sent to that API path (e.g. `/apis/myextension.mycompany.io/v1/…`) to the registered APIService.\"\n\n**What an APIService object registers:**\nAn APIService object \"claims\" the URL path in the Kubernetes API.\n\n**How it differs from CustomResourceDefinitions:**\n\"The aggregation layer is different from [Custom Resource Definitions](/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/api-extension/custom-resources/), which are a way to make the [kube-apiserver](/docs/concepts/architecture/#kube-apiserver \"Control plane component that serves the Kubernetes API.\") recognise new kinds of object.\"","durationMs":4462,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/api-extension/apiserver-aggregation/"}}
