{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JznbfxXnNFhi52eBqhAgsU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CNCF LFX Mentorship program contributor mentoring official page\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"LFX Mentorship – LFX Tools | Linux Foundation\",\"url\":\"https://lfx.linuxfoundation.org/tools/mentorship/\"},{\"title\":\"The mentorship flywheel: How CNCF is growing the next generation of cloud native leaders | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2025/12/18/the-mentorship-flywheel-how-cncf-is-growing-the-next-generation-of-cloud-native-leaders/\"},{\"title\":\"New Contributors | CNCF Contributors\",\"url\":\"https://contribute.cncf.io/contributors/\"},{\"title\":\"CNCF celebrates successful mentees from LFX Mentorship 2025 Term 3 | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/02/04/cncf-celebrates-successful-mentees-from-lfx-mentorship-2025-term-3/\"},{\"title\":\"Blog - CNCF Contributors - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\",\"url\":\"https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/\"},{\"title\":\"CNCF Mentorship 2026 Term 1 Project Proposals Open + GSoC 2026 Opportunities | CNCF Contributors\",\"url\":\"https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/2026/01/07/cncf-lfx-2026-term-1-announcement/\"},{\"title\":\"From mentee to mentor: my journey through LFX mentorship | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2023/07/11/from-mentee-to-mentor-my-journey-through-lfx-mentorship/\"},{\"title\":\"Strengthening LFX Mentoring: A Fairer Stipend Model and a Shared Standard of Excellence | CNCF Contributors\",\"url\":\"https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/2026/06/26/strengthening-lfx-mentoring/\"},{\"title\":\"Mentoring Programs and Resources | Kubernetes Contributors\",\"url\":\"https://www.kubernetes.dev/community/mentoring/\"},{\"title\":\"LFX Spring 2022 Mentorships are open - Apply for CNCF projects by February 13th! | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2022/02/09/lfx-spring-2022-internships-are-open-apply-for-cncf-projects-by-february-13th/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the CNCF LFX Mentorship program and contributor mentoring:\n\n## Key Information About CNCF LFX Mentorship Program\n\nThe CNCF LFX Mentorship program runs three terms per year, offering paid, three-month mentorship experiences across dozens of CNCF projects.\n\nLFX Mentoring has grown into one of the largest open source mentorship programs in the world, connecting new contributors with experienced maintainers across hundreds of projects every year.\n\n### Official Pages:\n\n1. **LFX Mentorship Platform**: https://lfx.linuxfoundation.org/tools/mentorship/\n2. **CNCF Contributors Page**: https://contribute.cncf.io/contributors/\n3. **CNCF Mentoring Blog and Resources**: https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/\n\n### Program Details:\n\nMentee stipends are calculated using a formula based on Purchasing Power Parity (PPP): starting from a $6,000 USD baseline and adjusting for each mentee's country.\n\nThe Cloud Native Computing Foundation participates in various mentoring programs, including LFX Mentorship (ex-CommunityBridge), a mentoring initiative by the Linux Foundation.\n\nFor more information or to explore available projects and apply, you can visit the official LFX Mentorship platform or the CNCF Contributors website.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"aeaa525c-563a-4e59-a75c-6c179656ab37","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:38:36.906Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"CNCF LFX Mentorship program contributor mentoring official page","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01XweJeuNx4oVEsjxvKgG1Wx","content":[{"title":"LFX Mentorship – LFX Tools | Linux Foundation","url":"https://lfx.linuxfoundation.org/tools/mentorship/"},{"title":"The mentorship flywheel: How CNCF is growing the next generation of cloud native leaders | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2025/12/18/the-mentorship-flywheel-how-cncf-is-growing-the-next-generation-of-cloud-native-leaders/"},{"title":"New Contributors | CNCF Contributors","url":"https://contribute.cncf.io/contributors/"},{"title":"CNCF celebrates successful mentees from LFX Mentorship 2025 Term 3 | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/02/04/cncf-celebrates-successful-mentees-from-lfx-mentorship-2025-term-3/"},{"title":"Blog - CNCF Contributors - Cloud Native Computing Foundation","url":"https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/"},{"title":"CNCF Mentorship 2026 Term 1 Project Proposals Open + GSoC 2026 Opportunities | CNCF Contributors","url":"https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/2026/01/07/cncf-lfx-2026-term-1-announcement/"},{"title":"From mentee to mentor: my journey through LFX mentorship | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2023/07/11/from-mentee-to-mentor-my-journey-through-lfx-mentorship/"},{"title":"Strengthening LFX Mentoring: A Fairer Stipend Model and a Shared Standard of Excellence | CNCF Contributors","url":"https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/2026/06/26/strengthening-lfx-mentoring/"},{"title":"Mentoring Programs and Resources | Kubernetes Contributors","url":"https://www.kubernetes.dev/community/mentoring/"},{"title":"LFX Spring 2022 Mentorships are open - Apply for CNCF projects by February 13th! | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2022/02/09/lfx-spring-2022-internships-are-open-apply-for-cncf-projects-by-february-13th/"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the CNCF LFX Mentorship program and contributor mentoring:\n\n## Key Information About CNCF LFX Mentorship Program\n\nThe CNCF LFX Mentorship program runs three terms per year, offering paid, three-month mentorship experiences across dozens of CNCF projects.\n\nLFX Mentoring has grown into one of the largest open source mentorship programs in the world, connecting new contributors with experienced maintainers across hundreds of projects every year.\n\n### Official Pages:\n\n1. **LFX Mentorship Platform**: https://lfx.linuxfoundation.org/tools/mentorship/\n2. **CNCF Contributors Page**: https://contribute.cncf.io/contributors/\n3. **CNCF Mentoring Blog and Resources**: https://contribute.cncf.io/blog/\n\n### Program Details:\n\nMentee stipends are calculated using a formula based on Purchasing Power Parity (PPP): starting from a $6,000 USD baseline and adjusting for each mentee's country.\n\nThe Cloud Native Computing Foundation participates in various mentoring programs, including LFX Mentorship (ex-CommunityBridge), a mentoring initiative by the Linux Foundation.\n\nFor more information or to explore available projects and apply, you can visit the official LFX Mentorship platform or the CNCF Contributors website."],"durationSeconds":6.36358070000005,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ErKsqNpFMti5RKKP8XUYmk","type":"tool_result","content":"Here are the exact quotes requested:\n\n**\"Vertical scaling\" bullet:**\n\"You can automatically scale a workload vertically using a [VerticalPodAutoscaler](/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/vertical-pod-autoscale/) (VPA). Unlike the HPA, the VPA doesn't come with Kubernetes by default, but is a an add-on that you or a cluster administrator may need to deploy before you can use it.\n\nOnce installed, it allows you to create [CustomResourceDefinitions](/docs/tasks/extend-kubernetes/custom-resources/custom-resource-definitions/ \"Custom code that defines a resource to add to your Kubernetes API server without building a complete custom server.\") (CRDs) for your workloads which define _how_ and _when_ to scale the resources of the managed replicas.\n\n#### Note:\n\nYou will need to have the [Metrics Server](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server) installed to your cluster for the VPA to work.\n\n#### In-place pod vertical scaling[](#in-place-pod-vertical-scaling)\n\nFeature state: Stable since Kubernetes v1.35\n\nMore information about this feature\n\nThis is a stable feature in Kubernetes, and has been since version v1.35. It was first available in the v1.27 release. You can no longer disable or opt out of this feature or behavior (it is locked); if you explicitly set a value for the associated feature gate InPlacePodVerticalScaling, Kubernetes ignores it but does not report any error.\n\nAs of Kubernetes 1.37, VPA does not support resizing pods in-place, but this integration is being worked on.\"\n\n**\"Event driven Autoscaling\" bullet:**\n\"It is also possible to scale workloads based on events, for example using the [_Kubernetes Event Driven Autoscaler_ (**KEDA**)](https://keda.sh/).\n\nKEDA is a CNCF-graduated project enabling you to scale your workloads based on the number of events to be processed, for example the amount of messages in a queue. There exists a wide range of adapters for different event sources to choose from.\"\n\n**\"Autoscaling based on schedules\" bullet:**\n\"Another strategy for scaling your workloads is to **schedule** the scaling operations, for example in order to reduce resource consumption during off-peak hours.\n\nSimilar to event driven autoscaling, such behavior can be achieved using KEDA in conjunction with its [`Cron` scaler](https://keda.sh/docs/latest/scalers/cron/). The `Cron` scaler allows you to define schedules (and time zones) for scaling your workloads in or out.\"\n\n**\"Scaling cluster infrastructure\" section:**\n\"If scaling workloads isn't enough to meet your needs, you can also scale your cluster infrastructure itself.\n\nScaling the cluster infrastructure normally means adding or removing [nodes](/docs/concepts/architecture/nodes/ \"A node is a worker machine in Kubernetes.\"). Read [Node autoscaling](/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/node-autoscaling/) for more information.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62653833-9f48-4f95-9546-6ca47ad80449","uuid":"23a74a27-3afa-4f43-8fcc-4fc9b3da1fec","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:38:37.244Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":502926,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"Here are the exact quotes requested:\n\n**\"Vertical scaling\" bullet:**\n\"You can automatically scale a workload vertically using a [VerticalPodAutoscaler](/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/vertical-pod-autoscale/) (VPA). Unlike the HPA, the VPA doesn't come with Kubernetes by default, but is a an add-on that you or a cluster administrator may need to deploy before you can use it.\n\nOnce installed, it allows you to create [CustomResourceDefinitions](/docs/tasks/extend-kubernetes/custom-resources/custom-resource-definitions/ \"Custom code that defines a resource to add to your Kubernetes API server without building a complete custom server.\") (CRDs) for your workloads which define _how_ and _when_ to scale the resources of the managed replicas.\n\n#### Note:\n\nYou will need to have the [Metrics Server](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server) installed to your cluster for the VPA to work.\n\n#### In-place pod vertical scaling[](#in-place-pod-vertical-scaling)\n\nFeature state: Stable since Kubernetes v1.35\n\nMore information about this feature\n\nThis is a stable feature in Kubernetes, and has been since version v1.35. It was first available in the v1.27 release. You can no longer disable or opt out of this feature or behavior (it is locked); if you explicitly set a value for the associated feature gate InPlacePodVerticalScaling, Kubernetes ignores it but does not report any error.\n\nAs of Kubernetes 1.37, VPA does not support resizing pods in-place, but this integration is being worked on.\"\n\n**\"Event driven Autoscaling\" bullet:**\n\"It is also possible to scale workloads based on events, for example using the [_Kubernetes Event Driven Autoscaler_ (**KEDA**)](https://keda.sh/).\n\nKEDA is a CNCF-graduated project enabling you to scale your workloads based on the number of events to be processed, for example the amount of messages in a queue. There exists a wide range of adapters for different event sources to choose from.\"\n\n**\"Autoscaling based on schedules\" bullet:**\n\"Another strategy for scaling your workloads is to **schedule** the scaling operations, for example in order to reduce resource consumption during off-peak hours.\n\nSimilar to event driven autoscaling, such behavior can be achieved using KEDA in conjunction with its [`Cron` scaler](https://keda.sh/docs/latest/scalers/cron/). The `Cron` scaler allows you to define schedules (and time zones) for scaling your workloads in or out.\"\n\n**\"Scaling cluster infrastructure\" section:**\n\"If scaling workloads isn't enough to meet your needs, you can also scale your cluster infrastructure itself.\n\nScaling the cluster infrastructure normally means adding or removing [nodes](/docs/concepts/architecture/nodes/ \"A node is a worker machine in Kubernetes.\"). Read [Node autoscaling](/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/node-autoscaling/) for more information.\"","durationMs":9387,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/autoscaling/"}}
